format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-half-life-group
statement: Know that half-life describes a group, not a timer in each nucleus.
misconception: A radioactive atom waits exactly one half-life before it decays.

item u2-l3-choice-buster
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    Which statement about half-life is true?

options:
    {"id":"group","label":"It describes how a group of nuclei decreases over time"}
    {"id":"timer","label":"It is a timer that runs inside each atom","note":"Half-life describes a group, not a timer in each atom."}
    {"id":"gone","label":"After one half-life the radioactivity is completely gone","note":"About half of the nuclei are still unstable."}
    {"id":"forever","label":"A radioactive material stays radioactive for ever","note":"Each radioactive material fades. Some do it fast and some very slowly."}

answer: group

explanation:
    Half-life describes how quickly a group of radioactive nuclei decreases. It is not a timer in each atom, and the radioactivity fades without being gone after one half-life.

item u2-l3-tom-crowd
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

statement:
    In a large group of nuclei, you can predict that about half will change in one half-life.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. One nucleus is unpredictable, but a large group follows a pattern.

item u2-l3-tom-timer
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

statement:
    A radioactive atom always waits exactly one half-life before it decays.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Half-life describes a group, not a timer in each atom. One nucleus might change soon or much later. Only the group follows the pattern.

item u2-x-tom-exact-one
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

statement:
    Half-life tells you exactly when one particular nucleus will decay.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Half-life describes a group. It cannot tell you when one nucleus will decay.

item u2-x-why-group
type: choice
difficulty: 2
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    Why can half-life describe a group but not one nucleus?

options:
    {"id":"pattern","label":"Each nucleus changes at a random moment, but a big group follows a pattern"}
    {"id":"timer","label":"Each nucleus has its own timer that is set to one half-life","note":"Half-life is not a timer in each atom."}
    {"id":"fewer","label":"A group has fewer nuclei, so it is easier to predict","note":"A larger group follows the pattern better, not a smaller one."}

answer: pattern

explanation:
    One nucleus is random, but a large group follows a pattern. That pattern is what half-life describes.
